{
  "requirement": "The review prompt MUST include at least one worked example of a mechanical change and at least one worked example of a design question. The mechanical example MUST show a flawed draft together with the change the reviewer makes to it.",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "**Obligations** (`requirements.cli.requirements_review_skill.review_prompt.examples`):\n1. The review prompt MUST include at least one worked example of a mechanical change.\n2. The mechanical example MUST show a flawed draft together with the change the reviewer makes to it.\n3. The review prompt MUST include at least one worked example of a design question.\n\n**Test.** `tests/authoring_cli.rs#fn[the_review_prompt_works_a_mechanical_example_and_a_design_question]` is marked `{test}` for this requirement. It takes the `## Examples` section of the skill's `references/review-prompt.md` through `section(review_prompt(), \"## Examples\")`. It then uses `assert_text_in_order` to require these phrases in order:\n- `Compound, with a mechanical fix:` introduces the mechanical example (obligation 1).\n- `> The \\`refactor\\` command accepts` is the quoted flawed draft.\n- `Split into` is the change the reviewer makes. Because the draft must appear before it, the test covers obligation 2, the draft together with the change.\n- `Not a question` marks the fix as mechanical.\n- `A design question:`, then `Options:`, then `Recommend` require a design-question example with options and a recommendation (obligation 3).\n\nI read the actual `Examples` section of `assets/skills/habeas-requirements/references/review-prompt.md`. It has a quoted compound draft and a \"Split into … Not a question.\" fix. It also has a second mechanical wording fix and a quoted design question with options (a)–(c) and a recommendation. The phrase \"Not a\\nquestion\" wraps across lines in the file. `section` returns a collapsed body (it ends with `collapse`), so the phrase matches. The in-order helper fails if any phrase is missing. Removing the draft, the fix or the design example would therefore fail the test.\n\n**Gaps.** I found none. The test doesn't assert the second mechanical example. The requirement asks for at least one, so that isn't a gap. Phrase-presence checks are an appropriate way to test a requirement about the content of a prompt document.",
